#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""
================================================================================
cleanclaude — surgical context reclamation for Claude Code conversations
================================================================================

A single-file experiment. It takes a Claude Code conversation (by id or a path
to its JSONL transcript), produces a smaller copy whose context has been
reclaimed — either by *pruning* heavy tool I/O in place, or by *partially
compacting* the older portion into a dense, role-structured summary — and then
proves the result by installing it as a NEW session and resuming it headlessly.

It is the inverse of Claude Code's "rewind": rewind drops the TAIL (undo recent
turns); cleanclaude shrinks the HEAD (forget old bulk) while keeping the recent
working set intact.

This docstring is the design record. It is deliberately long because the value
of this tool is less in the ~450 lines of code below it and more in *why* those
lines are shaped the way they are — a set of decisions earned by reading the
real serialization format, running real before/after experiments, and hitting
real failure modes. If you change the code, read this first; most "obvious
simplifications" re-break something documented here.


================================================================================
QUICKSTART
================================================================================

  # DEFAULT = COMPACTION: distill the oldest ~50% of context into role-structured turns,
  # keep the newest ~50% verbatim. Makes LLM calls. --ratio tunes how much:
  cleanclaude <session-id>                       # ratio 0.5  (0.25 ~ light · 0.75 ~ aggressive)
  cleanclaude <session-id> --ratio 0.75

  # --prune: the NO-LLM path — evict tool I/O to the snapshot store, leave <context-pruned> tags:
  cleanclaude <session-id> --prune

  # The exact command that created the 75%-reclaimed clone writing this docstring:
  cleanclaude fa7fe4db-4440-4bf9-b371-178fbdd5ee04 --ratio 0.75 --no-verify
  claude --resume <new-id>        # then resume the clone it prints (here: into tmux pane slomp:2.4)

  # Operate on a raw transcript path instead of an id (this is also the install/verify path):
  cleanclaude /path/to/transcript.jsonl

  What it prints: the source, the reduction %, validation, and the NEW session id it
  installed — then by default it resume-verifies. The original is NEVER touched; cleanclaude
  only ever writes a new-id copy and never deletes from ~/.claude/projects.

  --prune is lossless: every removed string is snapshotted to ~/.config/cleanclaude/sessions/<id>/
  and the <context-pruned> marker left behind points back at it (and at the live file) — nothing
  is deleted, only evicted from the context window.

  Finding a <session-id>: it is the <uuid> in ~/.claude/projects/<sanitized-cwd>/<uuid>.jsonl
  for the conversation. The newest-mtime .jsonl in that dir is usually the live one; the
  `agentName` field stamps which agent it belongs to; the get-history-filename skill (or
  `agent-message lookup <identity>`) resolves it.

  Flags: --ratio R (compaction: fold oldest R by weight; default 0.5) · --prune (no-LLM eviction) ·
  --keep-last N (prune: keep last N turns' tool I/O; default 4) · --chunk-tokens K · --no-verify.


================================================================================
1. THE PROBLEM, AND WHY THE OBVIOUS TOOLS DON'T SOLVE IT
================================================================================

A long agent session accumulates enormous, low-value bulk: file reads, command
output, search dumps, and the code the agent itself wrote and re-wrote. This
bulk dominates the token budget but is rarely load-bearing once the work has
moved on — the agent needed `settings.json`'s contents for two turns, not for
the next two hundred.

Claude Code already has two mechanisms here, and both are blunt:

  * `/compact` and auto-compaction (`autoCompactWindow`) summarize the ENTIRE
    conversation into one model-written blob when a token threshold is crossed.
    Three problems: (a) it fires on a raw token count, which can land mid-thought
    rather than at a clean boundary; (b) it summarizes EVERYTHING, including the
    recent turns you were actively leaning on; (c) it is all in the model's
    voice — the human's actual phrasing is paraphrased away (see §7, ENTROPY).

  * `/clear` throws the whole thing out.

What was missing is a *scalpel*: keep the recent working set verbatim, reclaim
the old bulk, and choose the boundary deliberately. That is cleanclaude.

An important non-goal: this is not trying to be Claude Code's compaction. It is
an external, offline editor of the serialized transcript that produces a fresh
resumable session. It can be far more surgical precisely because it is offline.


================================================================================
2. WHAT IT DOES (two modes + verify)
================================================================================

  cleanclaude <conversation-id>                 # COMPACTION — the default (makes LLM calls)
      Split the conversation into an old "head" and a recent "tail"; rebuild the
      head as a short run of role-structured summary turns (user verbatim,
      assistant distilled); keep the tail verbatim. --ratio sets the split.

  cleanclaude <conversation-id> --prune         # PRUNE — no LLM calls
      Evict the heavy tool I/O (to a content-addressed snapshot store) in every
      turn older than the keep window, leaving <context-pruned> tags in place.
      Keeps every turn; lossless; smaller reclaim than compaction.

  cleanclaude <path-to.jsonl>
      The load/verify path on a raw JSONL file: install it as a new session and
      resume-verify. (Both modes call this internally on their temp file; it is
      also usable standalone to "bring up" any transcript.)

  Both modes first EXCISE orphan tool_results (results whose tool_use was forked
  away by a /rewind — see §5), install a NEW session id, and end by RESUME-VERIFYING:
  the transcript is actually resumed headlessly, and success means it LOADED and the
  model responded — NOT that it emitted a particular string (a security-minded agent
  may rightly refuse a "say X" probe; that still counts as a successful load).

Flags:
  --ratio R          (default) compaction: fold the OLDEST R fraction of context by
                     weight into summary turns (default 0.5; newest 1-R kept verbatim)
  --prune            no-LLM eviction mode instead of compaction
  --keep-last N      prune: keep the last N user-prompt turns' tool I/O intact (default 4)
  --chunk-tokens K   compaction: summarize the head in ~K-token chunks (default 50000)
  --summarizer-model compaction: model that distills the assistant side
  --no-verify        skip the resume verification

Every run ends by ACTUALLY RESUMING the product (headless `-p`, with
`--no-session-persistence` so the probe turn doesn't pollute the clean session).
A transcript that validates statically but won't resume is a failure; the resume
is the real gate.


================================================================================
3. THE GROUND TRUTH: HOW A CLAUDE CODE TRANSCRIPT IS SERIALIZED
================================================================================

Everything below is a consequence of the on-disk format, which was learned by
inspecting real transcripts (not from docs). Get this wrong and nothing resumes.

  * Location: ~/.claude/projects/<sanitized-cwd>/<session-id>.jsonl, where the
    sanitized cwd is the working directory with every "/" replaced by "-"
    (/home/slomp -> -home-slomp). The mapping is lossy; confirm by mtime/content.

  * Format: JSON Lines — one JSON record per line. NOT a single JSON document.
    A live transcript's last line may be a partial write, so the loader skips
    un-parseable lines rather than crashing (see `load`).

  * It is a LINKED LIST (really a DAG). Each record has `uuid` and `parentUuid`.
    Resume does NOT read the file top-to-bottom; it finds the leaf (most recent)
    and walks `parentUuid` back to the root, reconstructing the thread. This is
    THE central fact. Consequences:
      - File order is not thread order. The file contains off-thread branches,
        sidechain (subagent) records, and meta records that are NOT on the live
        thread. Any analysis of "the conversation" must walk the chain, not the
        file (see `thread_records`). An early bug validated tool pairing in file
        order and produced false "orphan tool_result" errors from sidechains.
      - To delete a record you must SPLICE it: re-point its child's parentUuid to
        its parent. Orphan the chain and resume cannot rebuild the thread.

  * Record variety: the file is not just user/assistant messages. Real top-level
    `type`s seen include: custom-title, agent-name, last-prompt, mode,
    permission-mode, bridge-session, system, file-history-snapshot, attachment,
    user, assistant, plus rich metadata fields (sessionId, cwd, gitBranch,
    timestamp, requestId, isSidechain, isMeta, toolUseResult, leafUuid, ...).
    The leading non-message records are "scaffolding"; they sit on the chain and
    are preserved untouched.

  * Message content blocks: a `message.content` is either a string OR a list of
    blocks: {type:"text"}, {type:"tool_use", id, name, input}, {type:"tool_result",
    tool_use_id, content}, {type:"thinking", thinking, signature}.

  * TOOL PAIRING: an assistant `tool_use` (id) must be answered by a `tool_result`
    (tool_use_id) in the following user message. Drop one without the other and
    the API rejects the conversation. => prune in pairs, or stub content in place.

  * THINKING IS ALREADY STRIPPED. Real transcripts store thinking blocks but with
    EMPTY text — only the `signature` survives (171 blocks, 0 chars of thinking,
    171 signatures in the session this tool was built in). So thinking costs ~no
    tokens and is NOT a prune target. The signatures are required for API
    validity on resume, and you CANNOT edit a signed block without breaking it.
    => cleanclaude never touches thinking blocks.

  * toolUseResult: a top-level field that mirrors a tool's result as harness
    metadata, separate from the `message.content` tool_result the MODEL sees. It
    is large. We evict it too (it bloats the file), but note: it is NOT confirmed
    to be re-sent into the model's context on resume — so stubbing it may shrink
    the FILE more than the model's TOKEN context. The model-facing tool output is
    the `message.content` tool_result (~11% of file in our sample); toolUseResult
    is the rest of the bulk. (Open question, see §9.)

  * message.usage AND THE CONTEXT GAUGE (a caveat we did NOT foresee, now resolved).
    The context-% the harness shows on resume is read from the LAST `message.usage`
    block in the transcript — server-reported counts persisted from the prior turn,
    NOT a live recount. This bites every clone: a compacted/pruned copy inherits the
    PARENT's `usage` in its verbatim tail, so a session that is now a fraction of the
    real size is BORN reporting the parent's maxed-out gauge — and the harness will
    auto-compact away the very reduction we just made. Fix: `reset_usage()` runs in the
    install path and recursively zeroes every integer field whose key contains "token"
    (input_tokens, cache_read/creation_input_tokens, and the nested cache_creation.* /
    iterations.* counters) across all `usage` blocks. The next real turn writes accurate
    counts, so this ALIGNS the gauge with the true reduced size rather than faking it.
    Keep the distinction straight: cleanclaude does REAL token reduction; reset_usage
    only corrects the GAUGE so the harness believes it — and BOTH are needed, because a
    real reduction left under a stale gauge just gets auto-compacted away. (Discovered
    the hard way: a clone of this very session was born reading ~100% full and had to
    have its usage zeroed by hand before it would stop trying to auto-compact.)


================================================================================
4. THE INVARIANTS (break any of these and the result will not resume)
================================================================================

  I1. CHAIN WALKABILITY. leaf -> parentUuid -> ... -> root must be unbroken over
      the records actually written. Splice around deletions; re-link the first
      kept record to the last surviving ancestor.

  I2. TOOL PAIRING. Every tool_use on the live thread has its tool_result and
      vice versa. Stub-in-place preserves this trivially (no blocks removed);
      compaction preserves it by splitting only at user-prompt boundaries so no
      tool pair straddles the head/tail cut. CAVEAT: a real transcript may carry a
      PRE-EXISTING orphan — a tool_use forked away by a /rewind, or summarized off
      by native compaction. cleanclaude EXCISES these up front (excise_orphans: drop
      the orphan result, re-link the chain — exactly what Claude does on resume), so
      the produced thread is clean. validate() keeps an orphan check only as a
      backstop warning, never a block; the resume-verify is the real gate.

  I3. CONTENT IS AN ARRAY OF BLOCKS for synthetic records. Claude Code's resume
      code calls `message.content.some(...)` to scan for tool_use blocks; a bare
      string has no `.some` and throws ("content.some is not a function"). So any
      record we MANUFACTURE uses content: [{type:"text", text:...}], never a raw
      string. (Pre-existing string-content records are fine; only synthetic ones
      bit us.)

  I4. NO EMPTY message.content. If editing would empty a message, drop the whole
      record (and splice) rather than leave content: [].

  I5. DON'T TOUCH SIGNED THINKING BLOCKS. (See §3.)

  I6. KEEP THE SCAFFOLDING. The leading system/meta records are on the chain and
      resume expects them; re-link around message records, don't delete the
      plumbing.

  I7. DON'T TOUCH THE TAIL / THE LEAF. We always reclaim the head; the recent
      tail and the leaf are preserved verbatim.

`validate()` checks I1, I2, I4 along the reconstructed thread before any resume,
which catches most failures offline and cheaply.


================================================================================
5. PRUNE (--prune) — stub-in-place, made LOSSLESS by an eviction cache
================================================================================

Decision: STUB, don't EXCISE. Rather than removing records (which forces chain
surgery, pairing-aware deletion, and role-alternation care), we replace the
heavy *content* in place with a small marker, keeping every record, every uuid,
and every tool_use_id. The structure stays byte-valid; only the bulk leaves.
This is the 80/20: almost all the token weight is in a few content fields, and
shrinking them reclaims the vast majority for near-zero structural risk.

Three reclaim targets, each chosen deliberately:

  (a) tool_result bodies (message.content)  — the file reads / command output the
      model actually sees. THIS is the model-facing reclaim.
  (b) toolUseResult (top-level metadata)    — the harness's copy of the same; big
      in the file (see §3 caveat about whether it's in model context).
  (c) Edit/Write tool_use INPUT content     — old_string/new_string (Edit) and
      content (Write): the code the agent authored. Stripped while KEEPING
      file_path. (TOOL_INPUT_STRIP encodes which fields per tool.)

NOTHING IS LOST — IT'S EVICTED, NOT DELETED. Before a string is removed it is
written to a content-addressed SNAPSHOT STORE, and the marker left in its place
is a self-describing XML tag pointing at both copies:

  <context-pruned snapshot-of-original-content="<abs path>" original-file-path="<abs path>"/>

  * STORE: ~/.config/cleanclaude/sessions/<source-id>/<sha256(content)[:16]>
    (the sessions/ subdir keeps .config/cleanclaude free for other things and is
    easy to .gitignore). Keyed by the SOURCE conversation id — stable across runs,
    not the clone's new id — so a re-run reuses the existing files. Content-
    addressed: identical content (same file read twice, same stdout) stores once;
    `if not exists` makes re-runs idempotent.
  * TAG = the marker AND the instruction at once: XML the model parses natively,
    with attribute NAMES that explain themselves, no prose. Two self-contained
    recoveries: `cat` snapshot-of-original-content for exactly what was in context
    (immune to the live file drifting), or re-read original-file-path for the
    file's current state. original-file-path is present for tools that have one
    (Read/Edit/Write), absent for Bash/Grep (whose command/pattern is still
    visible in the adjacent tool_use).
  * IDEMPOTENCY: every tag starts with SENTINEL ("<context-pruned"), so a second
    pass recognizes its own prior work and skips it — re-runs are incremental
    (only new raw content is evicted) and never tag a tag.

So the original "forget the content, keep the pointer" became a true EVICTION
CACHE: the window is reclaimed, but every byte is recoverable, point-in-time, by
the path baked into each tag. The live pointer is still never touched.

That the content actually leaves the model's CONTEXT (not just the file) was
proven, not assumed:

  * Canary test (tool OUTPUT): a fresh agent read a file containing an unguessable
    token; with tools off, the control session recalled the token, the pruned
    session answered NOTAVAILABLE, and the token was grep-absent from the cleaned
    JSONL. Content gone from context; file_path preserved.
  * Canary test (Write INPUT): an agent invented a token and wrote it ONLY into a
    file (never said it). After input-stripping, the pruned session (tools off)
    answered NOTAVAILABLE while the path survived. Authored content left context;
    the pointer stayed. (Both predate the snapshot store — content is now also on
    disk, so "gone from context" no longer implies "gone for good".)

What is NOT pruned: conversation text (user + assistant prose), and thinking.


================================================================================
6. COMPACTION (the default) — role-structured, dual-voice
================================================================================

Pruning reclaims tool I/O but keeps the full turn-by-turn narrative. Partial
compaction goes further on the OLD portion: it summarizes it. But unlike native
compaction it (a) chooses the boundary, (b) keeps the recent tail 100% verbatim,
and (c) preserves the human's voice. Steps:

  STEP 1 — SPLIT into head (old) + tail (recent).
    By `--ratio R`: walk the conversation oldest-first by content weight; the
    head is the oldest records summing to ~R of total weight; the tail is the
    rest. (Or by `--keep-last N` turns.) The split ALWAYS lands on a user-prompt
    boundary so no tool pair straddles it (I2) and the tail begins with a real
    user turn. `--ratio` is the single knob worth tuning: 0.25 ≈ imperceptible,
    0.5 ≈ works well, 0.75 ≈ starts to get shaky (to be characterized).

  STEP 2 — CHUNK the head into ~`--chunk-tokens` segments at turn boundaries.
    WHY: a one-shot summary of a huge head is dicey. We learned this the hard
    way twice — a 235K-char head as a single `-p` ARGUMENT blew ARG_MAX
    ("Argument list too long"), which is why the summarizer now PIPES the head
    over STDIN; and big single summaries are lower quality / higher risk than a
    few focused ones. Chunking makes it robust and parallelizable.

  STEP 3 — DUAL-VOICE summarize each chunk, asymmetrically by role:
    * USER turns  -> KEPT VERBATIM. Concatenated as-is into a synthetic `user`
                     record. We do NOT paraphrase the human.
    * ASSISTANT turns -> DISTILLED by the model (`distill_assistant`, piped over
                     stdin) into a terse, first-person, model-voiced account of
                     what it did/decided. Synthetic `assistant` record.

  STEP 4 — REBUILD as REAL alternating user/assistant records (not a prose blob),
    fresh uuids, chained; splice: scaffolding + [user, assistant] x chunks + tail;
    re-link the first tail record to the last synthetic record.

  STEP 5 — validate, install, resume-verify (with the 1M model, because a large
    compacted context still needs the window).


================================================================================
7. THE LOAD-BEARING DECISIONS, AND WHY
================================================================================

7.1  WHY DUAL-VOICE, AND WHY THE ASYMMETRY IS INFORMATION-THEORETIC.
  The two roles carry different ENTROPY relative to the model that will read the
  summary. The assistant's own turns are LOW entropy to that model — it can
  regenerate its style and re-derive its conclusions from the outcomes; so the
  assistant side is safely distilled into the model's own voice. The human's
  turns are HIGH entropy — genuinely novel intent, phrasing, emphasis, and even
  the way the human floats an idea then walks it back; none of that is
  reconstructible. Compaction's usual quality loss is precisely that it renders
  EVERYTHING in the model's voice and flattens the human's signal. So the optimal
  allocation of the compression budget is: spend ZERO on the high-entropy human
  channel (keep it verbatim) and ALL of it on the low-entropy model channel.

  This was tested. At --ratio 0.5 on a real long session, the resumed clone:
    - reproduced the user's FIRST request near-verbatim ("...promoted to our own
      internal system... bypasses the auto classification... will you research
      that?") — voice preserved, because user turns were kept verbatim; and
    - accurately recalled a distilled technical fact (the dumbclaude vs tinyclaude
      subtractive/additive distinction) from ~80 turns back.

7.2  WHY REAL user/assistant RECORDS, NOT A DELIMITED PROSE BLOB.
  An earlier version embedded the summary as one prose blob with "===" segment
  separators. That is reinventing a serialization format the model has weak
  priors for. The `user`/`assistant` role container IS the model's NATIVE
  serialization: it is the chat template the model was post-trained on,
  ubiquitously. Representing recalled history in that structure is in-distribution
  — the model parses "these turns happened" with its strongest prior, no
  ad-hoc-delimiter tax. So the head is rebuilt as genuine alternating role
  records. (In the transcript case we can use REAL role fields, which is maximally
  native; a system-prompt-stuffing use case would fall back to textual "User:/
  Assistant:" markers, still strongly recognized.)

7.3  WHY VERBATIM USER, NOT A "VOICE-PRESERVING" USER COMPRESSOR.
  Tempting to summarize user turns with a "keep their voice" prompt. Rejected:
  any paraphrase is exactly the entropy destruction we are trying to avoid, and
  the reward is tiny because user turns are a small fraction of total tokens.
  Low reward, high risk. Verbatim wins. (FUTURE nuance, see §9: a user turn can
  contain pasted BULK — a log/doc/code dump — which is high-token and low-voice;
  the right lever there is a SIZE THRESHOLD that stubs only oversized user
  content, not a voice compressor.)

7.4  WHY KEEP THE RECENT TAIL VERBATIM (the attention argument, hedged).
  General transformer lore ("Lost in the Middle", Liu et al., ~2023) reports a
  U-shaped position bias: strong recall at the very start and the very end of a
  long context, weakest in the middle. If true for the models we target, the
  recent tail (high recency) and the original framing (high primacy) are the
  high-attention regions, and the middle is the cheapest to compress. HONESTY
  FLAG: that finding is from 2023-era models; current 1M-context models are
  trained to flatten that curve, so its transfer is UNVERIFIED. This claim was
  asserted from memory, not researched live — treat the attention story as
  motivation, not proof. What IS proven is the empirical result: keeping a
  generous verbatim tail + summarizing the old head produced a clone that
  continued with no apparent loss.

7.5  WHY CHUNK + STDIN. ARG_MAX (a 253K-char head as one argv arg fails); and
  smaller focused summaries beat one giant one. The summarizer reads the head on
  STDIN, with a short instruction in -p.

7.6  WHY THE SUMMARIZER RUNS WITH --tools "" --no-session-persistence. It only
  needs to read and write prose; no tools, and don't persist a throwaway session.

7.7  WHY VERIFY WITH --no-session-persistence AND THE 1M MODEL. The verify probe
  must not write a junk turn into the freshly-installed clean session, so it runs
  no-persistence. And a large pruned/compacted context can still exceed 200K, so
  the verify uses the 1M-window model to guarantee it loads.

7.8  WHY VALIDATE ALONG THE THREAD, NOT THE FILE. (See §3 — sidechains/branches.)

7.9  STUB OVER EXCISE (default). Excise (remove whole tool pairs + re-link) would
  reclaim the record skeletons too, but at the cost of pairing-aware deletion and
  chain surgery. Stub gets ~all the token weight for none of that risk. Excise is
  a future option (§9), not the default.

7.10 NON-DESTRUCTIVE BY CONSTRUCTION. cleanclaude NEVER deletes from
  ~/.claude/projects/. It reads, works on a temp copy, and WRITES new-id sessions.
  Each run leaves a new cleaned session behind (the deliverable). Nothing is
  overwritten or removed.


================================================================================
8. WHAT WAS ACTUALLY MEASURED (so the claims above aren't vibes)
================================================================================

  * Prune on a 1633-record session: 191 tool_result bodies + 122 Edit/Write input
    fields stubbed -> 36% smaller file; thread still valid + resumable.
  * Canary recall (output and input): content provably removed from the model's
    context (tools-off recall = NOTAVAILABLE), file_path provably preserved.
  * Partial compaction on a real ~85-turn, 5.4M-char session:
      - keep-last 3        -> 95% smaller; clone recalled early facts.
      - ratio 0.5 (chunked)-> 54-55% smaller; clone recalled an early technical
        distinction AND reproduced the user's verbatim framing.
  * Resume is verified by actually resuming and getting a response, every run.

  THE SELF-IDENTITY WRINKLE (a real finding, not a bug to fix): a resumed clone
  does NOT realize it has been compacted. It reads its verbatim tail — whose most
  recent content is "now" — and adopts that as its present moment and even its
  identity; in one run it insisted it was "the original, full-context session"
  and offered to go fetch the very result it WAS. The summary banner did not
  override the tail's salience. For CONTINUING work this temporal inheritance is
  a feature (you resume to keep going). For self-awareness it is a gotcha; a
  stronger banner or a system-prompt note would be needed to make the clone know
  it is a compaction artifact.


================================================================================
9. KNOWN LIMITATIONS & FUTURE TO-DOs (kept deliberately small — happy path)
================================================================================

  * STORE LIFECYCLE / GC: the snapshot store (§5) grows and is never cleaned. It's
    keyed by conversation id, so GC could tie to a conversation's deletion.
  * COMPACTION SNAPSHOTS: --prune evicts to the store; the default compaction still
    paraphrases the head's file-reads away without snapshotting them first. Natural extension.
  * User-content SIZE THRESHOLD: keep user turns verbatim (voice), but evict a
    user turn that is actually pasted BULK over N tokens. (§7.3.)
  * EXCISE mode: beyond excise_orphans, optionally remove whole tool pairs + re-link
    for deeper reclaim (the orphan excise already provides the re-link plumbing).
  * SELF-AWARENESS: optional stronger "you are resuming a compacted transcript"
    framing so the clone knows. (§8.)
  * toolUseResult ACCOUNTING: confirm whether it is re-sent to the model on
    resume; if not, evicting it is file-only, not token reclaim. (§3.)
  * "Lost in the middle" on CURRENT models: verify before relying on the
    middle-is-cheapest premise. (§7.4.)
  * Token sizing is a chars/4 proxy (CHARS_PER_TOKEN), not a real tokenizer.
  * NO BACKWARD COMPAT (by design): the on-disk tag + store layout isn't versioned,
    so transcripts pruned by an older cleanclaude won't be recognized on re-run.
    Fine for an experiment.


================================================================================
10. PHILOSOPHY: ONE HAPPY PATH
================================================================================

This is intentionally a single-file experiment with ONE evolving direction, not
a tool with a flag for every idea. When a better representation was found
(role-structured dual-voice), it REPLACED the prose-blob form rather than being
added behind a toggle. The cost of bespoke tools with many options is confusion;
the value is in settling on the happy path and sharpening it. The one real knob
is `--ratio` — how aggressive. Everything else has a defensible default.

A note on provenance, in the spirit of the project: claims in this file are
either (a) proven by an experiment described in §8, (b) read directly from the
serialization format (§3), or (c) flagged as asserted-from-memory and unverified
(§7.4, §3 toolUseResult). Don't let (c) masquerade as (a).
================================================================================
"""
import argparse, hashlib, json, os, re, subprocess, sys, tempfile, uuid
from pathlib import Path

PROJECTS = Path.home() / ".claude" / "projects"
STORE = Path.home() / ".config" / "cleanclaude" / "sessions"  # per-conversation snapshots, under a
# sessions/ subdir so .config/cleanclaude stays free for other things later (and is easy to .gitignore)
# heavy tool_use input fields to drop, keeping file_path (the breadcrumb) intact
TOOL_INPUT_STRIP = {"Write": ["content"], "Edit": ["old_string", "new_string"]}
CHARS_PER_TOKEN = 4  # rough proxy for token sizing

# ============================================================================
# MODEL-FACING TEXT — every string a model actually reads, gathered here as one
# editable overview. Two kinds: NOTES embedded into the produced transcript that
# the resumed agent reads, and PROMPTS sent to helper `claude -p` calls. The code
# below references these by name; tune wording/tone here.
# ============================================================================

# -- Marker left where content was removed: a self-explanatory, well-formed XML tag (no prose) --
# The resumed agent meets it right at the gap. The attribute NAMES are the instruction:
# `snapshot-of-original-content` is an absolute path to a frozen copy of exactly what was here (in
# this conversation's store); `original-file-path` (when known) is the live file on the current
# filesystem. Built by pruned_tag(); detected and parsed by TAG NAME + ATTRIBUTES, never by wording
# — so structure and prose can't drift apart.
#   e.g.  <context-pruned snapshot-of-original-content="/…/cleanclaude/sessions/<id>/<hash>" original-file-path="/abs/live/path"/>
PRUNED_TAG_NAME = "context-pruned"
SENTINEL = "<" + PRUNED_TAG_NAME

# Prepended to the first reconstructed turn of a compacted head:
COMPACT_BANNER = (
    "[The earlier part of this conversation is reconstructed below as compacted turns: your "
    "own assistant turns distilled to outcomes, the user's turns kept verbatim to preserve "
    "their voice. File paths named can be re-read on demand.]\n\n"
)

# -- Prompts sent to helper `claude -p` calls --
# Compaction dual-voice: distill one segment's assistant side into the model's own voice.
DISTILL_INSTRUCTION = (
    "The text on stdin is the ASSISTANT's side of part of a long agent coding conversation — its "
    "messages plus breadcrumbs of the tools it ran. Rewrite it in your own voice as a terse, "
    "first-person account of what you did, decided, and established. Keep file paths, identifiers, "
    "and concrete outcomes; drop retries, dead ends, and filler. Output only the account."
)

# summarize(): the alternate whole-head single-briefing prompt.
SUMMARIZE_INSTRUCTION = (
    "The text on stdin is the EARLIER portion of a long agent coding conversation. Compact it into "
    "a dense briefing so it can be dropped from context while the agent that continues the work keeps "
    "its understanding. Preserve: decisions made and why; facts, values, and identifiers established; "
    "file paths touched and what was done to each; the current state of the work; and any open threads "
    "or TODOs. Be terse; drop pleasantries, retries, and dead ends; never invent. Output only the briefing."
)

# install_and_verify(): headless resume-verify probe. We do NOT check WHAT the resumed agent says
# — a strong-persona or security-minded agent may (correctly) refuse a "reply with the exact string
# X" request as a prompt-injection pattern — only that it LOADED and responded without a transcript
# load error. So the probe is a neutral nudge, and success is judged by absence of load errors.
VERIFY_PROBE = "Context resume check — please reply briefly so we can confirm the session loaded."


def sanitize_cwd(cwd: str) -> str:
    return re.sub(r"/", "-", cwd)


def find_transcript(conv_id: str):
    hits = sorted(PROJECTS.glob(f"*/{conv_id}.jsonl"))
    return hits[0] if hits else None


def load(path) -> list:
    out = []
    for l in Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        if not l.strip():
            continue
        try:
            out.append(json.loads(l))
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            continue  # skip a partial/corrupt line (e.g. a live transcript's last write)
    return out


def dump(records: list, path):
    with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        for r in records:
            fh.write(json.dumps(r, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")


def is_user_prompt(rec: dict) -> bool:
    """A genuine user text prompt — not a tool_result-only message, not meta."""
    if rec.get("type") != "user":
        return False
    c = (rec.get("message") or {}).get("content")
    if isinstance(c, str):
        return bool(c.strip())
    if isinstance(c, list):
        return any(isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text" for b in c)
    return False


def snapshot(store_dir: Path, content) -> str:
    """Write the about-to-be-removed content to a content-addressed file in the per-conversation
    store and return its absolute path. Hashing the content makes this idempotent: identical
    content (the same file read twice, or a re-run of the tool) maps to the same file — written
    once, reused thereafter. The path is embedded in the <context-pruned> tag so the agent can `cat`
    the frozen snapshot even after the live file has drifted."""
    data = content if isinstance(content, str) else json.dumps(content, ensure_ascii=False)
    h = hashlib.sha256(data.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()[:16]
    store_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    f = store_dir / h
    if not f.exists():
        f.write_text(data, encoding="utf-8")
    return str(f)


def pruned_tag(snapshot_path: str, file_path=None) -> str:
    """Build the self-explanatory marker left where content was removed: a well-formed, self-closing
    XML tag (the model has strong priors for tags). The attribute NAMES are the instruction:
    `snapshot-of-original-content` = absolute path to a frozen copy of exactly what was here;
    `original-file-path` (when known) = the live file on the current filesystem. No prose
    instructions — the tag explains itself, detected/parsed by name + attributes, not wording."""
    attrs = f'snapshot-of-original-content="{snapshot_path}"'
    if file_path:
        attrs += f' original-file-path="{file_path}"'
    return f"<{PRUNED_TAG_NAME} {attrs}/>"


def stub_tool_results(content, store_dir: Path, useid_file: dict) -> int:
    n = 0
    if isinstance(content, list):
        for b in content:
            if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_result":
                cc = b.get("content")
                if isinstance(cc, str) and cc.startswith(SENTINEL):
                    continue  # already evicted on a prior run — skip (incremental re-run)
                b["content"] = pruned_tag(snapshot(store_dir, cc), useid_file.get(b.get("tool_use_id")))
                n += 1
    return n


def stub_tool_inputs(content, store_dir: Path) -> int:
    """Drop heavy tool_use input fields (Edit old/new strings, Write content) while keeping
    file_path (the breadcrumb) intact. The removed content is snapshotted to the store first,
    so it stays recoverable point-in-time."""
    n = 0
    if isinstance(content, list):
        for b in content:
            if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_use":
                inp = b.get("input") or {}
                fp = inp.get("file_path")
                for k in TOOL_INPUT_STRIP.get(b.get("name"), []):
                    if k in inp:
                        v = inp[k]
                        if isinstance(v, str) and v.startswith(SENTINEL):
                            continue
                        inp[k] = pruned_tag(snapshot(store_dir, v), fp)
                        n += 1
    return n


def prune(records: list, keep_last: int, store_dir: Path):
    """Stub tool_result bodies in every record before the cutoff. Cutoff = the start of the
    last `keep_last` genuine user prompts (those turns stay intact). Each removed string is
    snapshotted to store_dir first, so nothing is truly lost — only evicted from the window."""
    useid_file = {}  # tool_use id -> live file_path, so a result's tag can name the live file
    for r in records:
        c = (r.get("message") or {}).get("content")
        if isinstance(c, list):
            for b in c:
                if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_use":
                    fp = (b.get("input") or {}).get("file_path")
                    if fp:
                        useid_file[b.get("id")] = fp
    prompts = [i for i, r in enumerate(records) if is_user_prompt(r)]
    if len(prompts) > keep_last:
        cutoff = prompts[-keep_last]
    else:
        cutoff = len(records)  # nothing old enough to prune
    n_res = n_inp = 0
    for i, r in enumerate(records):
        if i >= cutoff:
            continue
        content = (r.get("message") or {}).get("content")
        n_res += stub_tool_results(content, store_dir, useid_file)
        n_inp += stub_tool_inputs(content, store_dir)
        tur = r.get("toolUseResult")
        if tur is not None and not (isinstance(tur, str) and tur.startswith(SENTINEL)):
            rfile = None
            if isinstance(content, list):
                for b in content:
                    if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_result":
                        rfile = useid_file.get(b.get("tool_use_id"))
                        break
            r["toolUseResult"] = pruned_tag(snapshot(store_dir, tur), rfile)
    return records, n_res, n_inp, cutoff


def thread_records(records: list) -> list:
    """Reconstruct the actual conversation thread the way resume does: start at
    the leaf (most recent uuid) and walk parentUuid back to the root. This skips
    off-thread / sidechain / branched records that aren't in the live context."""
    by_uuid = {r["uuid"]: r for r in records if r.get("uuid")}
    leaf = next((r["uuid"] for r in reversed(records) if r.get("uuid")), None)
    out, seen, u = [], set(), leaf
    while u and u in by_uuid and u not in seen:
        seen.add(u)
        out.append(by_uuid[u])
        u = by_uuid[u].get("parentUuid")
    out.reverse()
    return out


def validate(records: list) -> tuple:
    """Static checks along the live thread. Returns (errors, warnings, thread_len). Orphan
    tool_results are WARNINGS, not errors: a real transcript can carry pre-existing orphans (a
    tool_use summarized away by native compaction, or left on a rewound/branched path), and Claude
    Code resumes them fine by filtering on resume. A stub-prune removes no records so it cannot
    introduce one — an orphan must NOT block. The resume-verify is the real gate."""
    errs, warns = [], []
    thread = thread_records(records)
    seen_use = set()
    for r in thread:
        msg = r.get("message") or {}
        c = msg.get("content")
        if isinstance(c, list):
            for b in c:
                if not isinstance(b, dict):
                    continue
                if b.get("type") == "tool_use":
                    seen_use.add(b.get("id"))
                elif b.get("type") == "tool_result" and b.get("tool_use_id") not in seen_use:
                    warns.append(f"orphan tool_result {b.get('tool_use_id')} (pre-existing)")
            if msg.get("role") and c == []:
                errs.append(f"empty content at {str(r.get('uuid',''))[:8]}")
    return errs, warns, len(thread)


def excise_orphans(records: list):
    """Drop tool_results on the live thread whose tool_use was forked away (e.g. by /rewind) and
    re-link the chain. Such a result's call sits on an abandoned branch — never an ancestor — so it
    is meaningless to the model, and Claude itself drops these on resume. Removing them here keeps
    the output API-clean without leaning on resume-time filtering. Returns (records, n_excised)."""
    thread = thread_records(records)
    use_ids = set()
    for r in thread:
        c = (r.get("message") or {}).get("content")
        if isinstance(c, list):
            for b in c:
                if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_use":
                    use_ids.add(b.get("id"))
    orphan_uuids = set()
    for r in thread:
        c = (r.get("message") or {}).get("content")
        if isinstance(c, list) and c:
            blocks = [b for b in c if isinstance(b, dict)]
            results = [b for b in blocks if b.get("type") == "tool_result"]
            # a record that is PURELY orphan tool_results -> excise the whole record
            if results and len(results) == len(blocks) and all(b.get("tool_use_id") not in use_ids for b in results):
                orphan_uuids.add(r.get("uuid"))
    if not orphan_uuids:
        return records, 0
    parent = {r.get("uuid"): r.get("parentUuid") for r in records}
    def live_ancestor(u):
        seen = set()
        while u in orphan_uuids and u not in seen:
            seen.add(u)
            u = parent.get(u)
        return u
    out = []
    for r in records:
        if r.get("uuid") in orphan_uuids:
            continue
        if r.get("parentUuid") in orphan_uuids:
            r["parentUuid"] = live_ancestor(r.get("parentUuid"))
        out.append(r)
    return out, len(orphan_uuids)


def reset_usage(records: list) -> int:
    """Zero the persisted token counters so a freshly-installed clone reports its TRUE
    (reduced) size on resume. The context gauge reads the last message.usage block, not a
    live recount — and a clone inherits the PARENT's usage in its verbatim tail, so it is born
    overstating itself and threatening to auto-compact away the reduction we just made. We
    recursively zero every integer field whose key contains "token" (catching the nested
    cache_creation.* and iterations.* counters). The next real turn writes accurate counts, so
    this is a one-time reset that aligns the gauge with the real size, not a permanent fake."""
    def zero(obj):
        if isinstance(obj, dict):
            for k, v in obj.items():
                if isinstance(v, bool):
                    continue
                if isinstance(v, int) and "token" in k.lower():
                    obj[k] = 0
                elif isinstance(v, (dict, list)):
                    zero(v)
        elif isinstance(obj, list):
            for v in obj:
                zero(v)
    n = 0
    for r in records:
        u = (r.get("message") or {}).get("usage")
        if isinstance(u, dict):
            zero(u)
            n += 1
        if isinstance(r.get("usage"), dict):
            zero(r["usage"])
    return n


def install_and_verify(records: list, do_verify: bool):
    """Write records as a NEW session under the CURRENT working directory (where cleanclaude was
    invoked) — NOT the source session's original cwd — and resume it headlessly without persisting
    the probe turn. `claude --resume` is cwd-scoped (it looks only in the project dir for the cwd it
    runs from), so installing under the caller's cwd means the clone resumes from where YOU are,
    instead of some deep directory the original agent happened to be working in. Every record's cwd
    is restamped to match."""
    launch_cwd = os.getcwd()
    new_id = str(uuid.uuid4())
    for r in records:
        if "sessionId" in r:
            r["sessionId"] = new_id
        if "cwd" in r:
            r["cwd"] = launch_cwd
    proj = PROJECTS / sanitize_cwd(launch_cwd)
    proj.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    target = proj / f"{new_id}.jsonl"
    n_usage = reset_usage(records)
    if n_usage:
        print(f"usage  : zeroed token counters in {n_usage} usage block(s) — clone reports its true size on resume")
    dump(records, target)
    if not do_verify:
        return None, new_id, target
    probe = subprocess.run(
        ["claude", "--resume", new_id, "--no-session-persistence", "--strict-mcp-config",
         "--model", "claude-opus-4-8[1m]", "-p", VERIFY_PROBE],
        cwd=launch_cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=300,
    )
    # Resume succeeded if it LOADED and the model produced ANY response without a transcript load
    # error. We don't require specific output — see VERIFY_PROBE.
    blob = ((probe.stdout or "") + "\n" + (probe.stderr or "")).lower()
    load_errors = ("no conversation found", "is not a function", "not well-formed",
                   "argument list too long", "unexpected token", "cannot read propert")
    ok = probe.returncode == 0 and bool((probe.stdout or "").strip()) and not any(e in blob for e in load_errors)
    return (ok, probe), new_id, target


def render_head(head: list) -> str:
    """Render the head turns compactly for the summarizer: full user/assistant text,
    tool calls as breadcrumbs (name + path/command), results truncated."""
    lines = []
    for r in head:
        msg = r.get("message") or {}
        role = (msg.get("role") or "").upper()
        c = msg.get("content")
        if isinstance(c, str):
            if c.strip():
                lines.append(f"{role}: {c.strip()}")
        elif isinstance(c, list):
            for b in c:
                if not isinstance(b, dict):
                    continue
                t = b.get("type")
                if t == "text" and (b.get("text") or "").strip():
                    lines.append(f"{role}: {b['text'].strip()}")
                elif t == "tool_use":
                    inp = b.get("input") or {}
                    coord = inp.get("file_path") or inp.get("command") or inp.get("pattern") or ""
                    lines.append(f"  [tool {b.get('name')}: {str(coord)[:120]}]")
                elif t == "tool_result":
                    rc = b.get("content")
                    rc = rc if isinstance(rc, str) else json.dumps(rc)
                    lines.append(f"  [result: {rc[:150].strip()}]")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def summarize(head_text: str, model: str) -> str:
    """Pipe the head to a Claude -p over stdin (avoids ARG_MAX) and get a dense briefing."""
    p = subprocess.run(
        ["claude", "--strict-mcp-config", "--no-session-persistence", "--tools", "",
         "--model", model, "-p", SUMMARIZE_INSTRUCTION],
        input=head_text, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=420,
    )
    return (p.stdout or "").strip()


def chunk_head(head: list, chunk_tokens: int) -> list:
    """Split the head into ~chunk_tokens chunks, breaking only at turn boundaries
    (a turn starts at each genuine user prompt) so each chunk is coherent."""
    budget = max(chunk_tokens, 1000) * CHARS_PER_TOKEN
    turns, cur = [], []
    for r in head:
        if is_user_prompt(r) and cur:
            turns.append(cur)
            cur = [r]
        else:
            cur.append(r)
    if cur:
        turns.append(cur)
    chunks, c, c_chars = [], [], 0
    for turn in turns:
        tchars = len(render_head(turn))
        if c and c_chars + tchars > budget:
            chunks.append(c)
            c, c_chars = [], 0
        c.extend(turn)
        c_chars += tchars
    if c:
        chunks.append(c)
    return chunks


def _make_record(role: str, text: str, parent_uuid, template: dict) -> dict:
    r = {"type": role, "uuid": str(uuid.uuid4()), "parentUuid": parent_uuid,
         "message": {"role": role, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": text}]}, "sessionId": ""}
    if template.get("cwd"):
        r["cwd"] = template["cwd"]
    if template.get("timestamp"):
        r["timestamp"] = template["timestamp"]
    return r


def split_chunk_voices(chunk: list) -> tuple:
    """Separate a chunk into the user's VERBATIM text (preserved — high-entropy signal)
    and a render of the assistant's side (to be distilled into the model's own voice)."""
    user_parts, asst_render = [], []
    for r in chunk:
        msg = r.get("message") or {}
        role, c = msg.get("role"), msg.get("content")
        if role == "user":
            if isinstance(c, str):
                if c.strip():
                    user_parts.append(c.strip())
            elif isinstance(c, list):
                for b in c:
                    if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text" and (b.get("text") or "").strip():
                        user_parts.append(b["text"].strip())
        elif role == "assistant":
            asst_render.append(render_head([r]))
    return "\n\n".join(user_parts), "\n".join(a for a in asst_render if a.strip())


def distill_assistant(asst_text: str, model: str) -> str:
    """Distill the assistant's side into a terse first-person account in the model's own voice."""
    if not asst_text.strip():
        return "(no assistant activity in this segment)"
    p = subprocess.run(
        ["claude", "--strict-mcp-config", "--no-session-persistence", "--tools", "",
         "--model", model, "-p", DISTILL_INSTRUCTION],
        input=asst_text, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=420,
    )
    return (p.stdout or "").strip() or "(assistant activity omitted)"


def compact(records: list, keep_last: int, ratio, chunk_tokens: int, model: str):
    """Partial compaction, role-structured + dual-voice. The head (oldest portion) is rebuilt
    as real alternating user/assistant records: user turns kept VERBATIM (high-entropy signal,
    cheap), assistant turns DISTILLED into the model's own voice per ~chunk_tokens segment. The
    tail is kept verbatim. Split by --ratio of context weight (oldest fraction) else keep_last."""
    thread = thread_records(records)
    prompts = [i for i, r in enumerate(thread) if is_user_prompt(r)]
    if len(prompts) < 2:
        return None
    first = prompts[0]
    conv = thread[first:]
    lprompts = [i - first for i in prompts]  # prompt indices relative to conv
    if ratio is not None:
        weights = [len(json.dumps((r.get("message") or {}).get("content") or "")) for r in conv]
        cumw = [0]
        for w in weights:
            cumw.append(cumw[-1] + w)
        target = cumw[-1] * ratio
        cands = [p for p in lprompts if p > 0 and cumw[p] >= target]
        split = cands[0] if cands else lprompts[-1]
    else:
        if len(lprompts) <= keep_last:
            return None
        split = lprompts[-keep_last]
    if split <= 0:
        return None
    scaffolding = thread[:first]
    head, tail = conv[:split], conv[split:]
    if not head or not tail:
        return None
    template = next((r for r in tail if r.get("cwd")), tail[0])
    chunks = chunk_head(head, chunk_tokens)
    new_head = []
    prev = scaffolding[-1]["uuid"] if scaffolding else None
    for i, ch in enumerate(chunks):
        user_text, asst_text = split_chunk_voices(ch)
        distilled = distill_assistant(asst_text, model)
        if i == 0:
            user_text = COMPACT_BANNER + user_text
        u = _make_record("user", user_text or "(no user text in this segment)", prev, template)
        prev = u["uuid"]
        a = _make_record("assistant", distilled, prev, template)
        prev = a["uuid"]
        new_head += [u, a]
    tail[0]["parentUuid"] = prev
    new_records = scaffolding + new_head + tail
    stats = {"chunks": len(chunks), "synthetic_records": len(new_head),
             "head_records": len(head), "tail_records": len(tail)}
    return new_records, stats


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="cleanclaude", description="Reclaim context from a Claude Code session — compaction by default, or --prune — and verify it resumes.")
    ap.add_argument("target", help="conversation id, or a path to a .jsonl transcript")
    ap.add_argument("--prune", action="store_true", help="prune instead of compacting: evict tool I/O to the snapshot store and leave <context-pruned> tags — NO LLM calls; keeps every turn")
    ap.add_argument("--ratio", type=float, default=0.5, help="compaction: fold up the oldest fraction of context by weight (0.5 = compact ~50%%, keep newest ~50%% verbatim)")
    ap.add_argument("--keep-last", type=int, default=4, help="prune: keep the last N user-prompt turns' tool I/O intact (default 4)")
    ap.add_argument("--summarizer-model", default="claude-sonnet-4-6", help="compaction: model that distills the assistant side")
    ap.add_argument("--chunk-tokens", type=int, default=50000, help="compaction: summarize the head in chunks of about this many tokens (default 50000)")
    ap.add_argument("--no-verify", action="store_true", help="skip the resume verification")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    is_path = args.target.endswith(".jsonl") or os.path.isfile(args.target)

    if is_path:
        src = Path(args.target)
        if not src.is_file():
            print(f"cleanclaude: no such file: {src}", file=sys.stderr); return 1
        records = load(src)
        records, n_orphan = excise_orphans(records)
        print(f"loaded {len(records)} records from {src}" + (f"; excised {n_orphan} orphan tool_result(s)" if n_orphan else ""))
    else:
        src = find_transcript(args.target)
        if not src:
            print(f"cleanclaude: no transcript found for id {args.target}", file=sys.stderr); return 1
        print(f"source : {src}")
        records = load(src)
        before = sum(len(json.dumps(r)) for r in records)
        records, n_orphan = excise_orphans(records)
        if n_orphan:
            print(f"excised: {n_orphan} orphan tool_result(s) from rewound/abandoned branch(es)")
        if args.prune:
            store_dir = STORE / args.target
            records, n_res, n_inp, cutoff = prune(records, args.keep_last, store_dir)
            after = sum(len(json.dumps(r)) for r in records)
            tmp = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cleanclaude-")) / f"pruned-{args.target}.jsonl"
            dump(records, tmp)
            print(f"pruned : stubbed {n_res} tool_result bodies + {n_inp} Edit/Write input fields before record {cutoff} (kept last {args.keep_last} prompts)")
            print(f"snapshots: point-in-time content saved under {store_dir}")
        else:
            res = compact(records, args.keep_last, args.ratio, args.chunk_tokens, args.summarizer_model)
            if res is None:
                print("nothing to compact (too few turns, or ratio leaves nothing)"); return 0
            records, stats = res
            after = sum(len(json.dumps(r)) for r in records)
            tmp = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cleanclaude-")) / f"compacted-{args.target}.jsonl"
            dump(records, tmp)
            print(f"compact: {stats['head_records']} head records -> {stats['chunks']} segments = {stats['synthetic_records']} role records (user verbatim, assistant distilled); kept {stats['tail_records']} tail verbatim [ratio {args.ratio}]")
        print(f"size   : {before:,} -> {after:,} chars  ({100*(before-after)//max(before,1)}% smaller)")
        print(f"temp   : {tmp}")

    errs, warns, tlen = validate(records)
    if errs:
        print(f"VALIDATION FAILED ({len(errs)}): " + "; ".join(errs[:5]), file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    note = f" — {len(warns)} pre-existing orphan tool_result(s), tolerated (Claude filters on resume)" if warns else ""
    print(f"validate: live thread {tlen} records — chain + tool-pairing + no-empty OK{note}")

    result, new_id, target = install_and_verify(records, do_verify=not args.no_verify)
    print(f"installed new session: {new_id}")
    print(f"           {target}")
    if result is None:
        print("verify : skipped (--no-verify)")
        return 0
    ok, probe = result
    if ok:
        print(f"verify : RESUMED OK — session loaded into a fresh context ✓")
        print(f"resume (from {os.getcwd()}):  claude --resume {new_id}")
        return 0
    print("verify : FAILED to resume", file=sys.stderr)
    print((probe.stderr or probe.stdout or "")[-600:], file=sys.stderr)
    return 3


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
